.\" ezmk-workspace.toml(5) - EazyMake workspace configuration file (1.4.3)
.TH EZMK\-WORKSPACE.TOML 5 "2026-09-26" "EazyMake 1.4.4" "File Formats"
.nh
.SH NAME
ezmk\-workspace.toml \- EazyMake workspace configuration file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
[workspace]
name = "my\-ws"
members = ["apps/tool\-a", "libs/strutil"]

[workspace.options]
default_jobs = 4
stop_on_error = false
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
A workspace is a collection of independent projects (called
.IR members )
that live under one directory. The file
.B ezmk\-workspace.toml
declares the member list and the workspace\-wide options, and the
.B ezmk workspace
commands then operate on every member in dependency order.
.PP
The file is
.B independent of
.BR ezmk.toml :
each member keeps its own
.B ezmk.toml
with its own project settings, and a workspace root may itself be a project
as well (a directory holding both files is the workspace root
.I and
a member).
.PP
The workspace root is located by searching
.B upward
from the current directory, so every
.B ezmk workspace
command (and the
.B \-w
redirect of
.BR "build" ", " "test" ", " "watch" ", " "clean" )
works from any subdirectory. A directory that contains only
.B ezmk\-workspace.toml
is a
.IR "pure container root" :
.B ezmk build
there prints a hint to use
.B ezmk workspace build
(or
.BR "ezmk build \-w" )
instead of failing.
.SH WORKSPACE FILE
.SS The [workspace] table
.TP
.B name
Optional workspace name (string). Used for display only; it does not have to
match the directory name and is preserved verbatim by
.BR "ezmk workspace scan" .
.TP
.B members
Required, non\-empty array of member directories, relative to the workspace
root. Entries must exist, must contain their own
.BR ezmk.toml ,
and must not contain a nested
.B ezmk\-workspace.toml
(nested workspaces are rejected). A member may also be listed by a
.I basename
elsewhere on the command line, see
.B \-\-member
in
.BR ezmk (1).
.SS The [workspace.options] table
.TP
.B default_jobs
Non\-negative integer. Default
.BR 0 ,
which means
.IR "auto" :
use the hardware concurrency. It sets the workspace\-wide parallelism for
.B workspace build
and
.BR "workspace test" ;
an explicit
.B \-j
on the command line wins over it.
.TP
.B stop_on_error
Boolean, default
.BR false .
When
.BR true ,
.B workspace build
and
.B workspace test
stop dispatching new member tasks after the first failure: members that have
not started yet are reported as
.IR skipped ,
while members already running finish naturally (they are never killed).
.B workspace clean
has no dependency semantics and rejects the flag.
.PP
Unknown keys inside either table are rejected with an error naming the
offending key, so typos fail loudly instead of being ignored. Type errors
(e.g. a string where an array is expected) are reported with the file path.
.SH MEMBER DEPENDENCIES
A member declares a dependency on a sibling member of the
.I same
workspace with the
.B workspace
key of its own
.BR [depends] ,
in its own
.BR ezmk.toml :

.nf
[depends]
workspace = ["strutil"]      # basename, or "libs/strutil" (relative path)
.fi

Rules:
.IP \(bu 2
The graph must be
.BR "one\-way acyclic" ;
cycles and self\-loops are rejected when the configuration is loaded.
.IP \(bu 2
The depended\-upon member must be
.BR "type = \(dqstatic\(dq" ;
its
.B build/lib<name>.a
is reused. No version constraints exist: this is develop\-and\-use against
source, not a package dependency (version constraints belong to the package
system, see
.B ezmk pkg
in
.BR ezmk (1)).
.IP \(bu 2
Sibling artifacts are injected automatically during the member's own build:
.B \-I
.IB <ws>/<member>/include
(only when it exists) and
.B \-L
.IB <ws>/<member>/build
.B \-l<member>
(only when the static library exists). No environment variable is involved,
so injection does not grow with workspace size.
.IP \(bu 2
The injected include path is part of the compile signature: changing a
library header recompiles its dependents, while changing a library source
only relinks them.
.PP
When a sibling artifact is missing (for example in a standalone build inside
one member), the build prints a hint to run
.B ezmk workspace build
first and continues; a link error follows naturally if the artifact is really
needed.
.SH COMMANDS
The workspace commands are documented in
.BR ezmk (1),
section
.BR COMMANDS :
.BR "ezmk workspace list" ,
.BR build ,
.BR test ,
.BR watch ,
.BR clean
and
.BR scan .
The most important interactions with this file are the parallelism precedence
(explicit
.B \-j
overrides
.BR default_jobs ,
which overrides hardware concurrency), the
.B \-\-member
target plus dependency closure, and the
.B \-w
redirect that turns a project command into its workspace equivalent.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B ezmk\-workspace.toml
The workspace root file described here.
.TP
.B <member>/ezmk.toml
Per\-member project configuration; the
.B [depends]
.B workspace
key is read from there.
.TP
.BR .ezmk/ " and " build/
Per\-member state directories; workspace commands do not share them between
members.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ezmk (1),
.BR ezmk.toml (5)
.PP
Full specification:
.IR https://github.com/3667808244/EazyMake
(section
.B workspace
of
.IR docs/en/cli.md ,
and
.B depends
of
.IR docs/en/config_file.md ).
